# What this directory is

`.converge/` is where a **manager session** keeps the state of a running
operation on this workspace. Copy this file to `<workspace>/.converge/README.md`
when you create the directory.

Nothing in here is the project's content. It is a running operation's scratch
space: delete a finished operation's directory and the project is untouched.

## The shape

```
<workspace>/.converge/
  README.md              <- this file
  <manager-id>/          <- one directory per project the workspace runs
    registration.toml    who this manager session is, rewritten on every wake
    HIGHWAY.md           the plan record - the operating picture, rewritten each cycle
    manifest.tsv         which lane is on which branch, written by the tooling
    goals/               one pre-composed brief per queued item
    lanes/               one git worktree per lane, each on its own branch
    infra.tsv            the infra ledger - anything stood up, with its teardown command
    infra.owners.tsv     which lane claimed which infra row
    .width               the lane count the steward set
    watchdog.log         the loop that re-wakes the manager session
```

`<manager-id>` is the project's own name - the one its work queue carries. Two
projects run from the same workspace get two directories and share nothing.

`registration.toml` is how the companion app finds this operation at all. The
manager session rewrites it on **every** wake - its name, steward, workspace
root, repositories with their default branches, plan record, tmux socket and
session, work queue, and the time of that wake - and the app scans the workspace
roots it knows for exactly this file. Nobody registers a manager session by hand,
and an operation that started before any of this appears the next time it wakes.
The stamp is also the heartbeat: past fifteen minutes with no rewrite, the app
says the session is silent and may have died. Delete it and the operation simply
stops being listed; the next wake writes it again.

## Why here and not under your home directory

A manager session runs from the **workspace root**: the nearest ancestor
directory holding `WORKSPACE-MANIFEST.json`, or the project repository's own root
when there is none. Everything it stands up lives under `.converge/` beneath that
root, so an operation never lands outside the project it serves.

The alternative was measured and it is worse. Batch directories created under
`~/dev/hw-<name>` outlive the workspace they belonged to, and nothing in the home
directory says which project any of them served - so nobody can tell a live
operation from an abandoned one, and nobody can safely sweep either.

## Git

This whole directory is git-ignored, this file included. Add the line from
`docs/workspace-template/gitignore-addendum.txt` to the workspace's `.gitignore`
when you create it.

Ignoring it is deliberate: worktrees, logs, and a ledger of live infrastructure
are state, not content, and committing them makes every cycle a diff nobody
reads. The tracked copy of this explanation is the template it came from, not
this instance of it.

## Closing an operation

Sweep the infra ledger first - it is the only record of anything the run stood up
outside this directory - and only then delete `<manager-id>/`. A directory
removed before its ledger is swept leaves live infrastructure with no teardown
command anywhere.
